        "id": "teaching-philosophy",
        "label": "Teaching Philosophy",
        "title": "My Teaching Philosophy",
        "eyebrow": "What guides my practice",
        "intro": "Learners at the centre. Always.",
        "body": "My teaching philosophy is based on the belief that effective English language teaching should create a supportive, engaging, and learner-centred environment in which students feel comfortable participating, making mistakes  and developing their abilities. During my six week teaching practicum at Gurukula National College, Kelaniya, I taught English to Grade 6 and grade 9 students. This experience helped me understand that teaching involves more than explaining grammatical rules; it also requires understanding learners’ needs, encouraging participation and creating meaningful learning experiences.\n\nI believe that a friendly and supportive teacher can positively influence students’ attitudes towards learning English. Rather than maintaining a strictly authoritative classroom atmosphere, I tried to establish positive relationships with my students while maintaining appropriate classroom expectations. I wanted them to feel comfortable asking questions, expressing their ideas and learning from their mistakes. This approach reflects the humanistic perspective of education, which emphasises learners’ feelings, individual needs and personal development (Rogers, 1969). My practicum helped me recognise that students are more likely to participate actively when they feel respected, encouraged and supported.\nAnother important aspect of my teaching philosophy is the use of interactive activities to promote learning. Instead of relying entirely on written notes, I incorporated pair work, group activities and individual tasks, particularly when teaching grammar. These activities provided opportunities for students to practise language, exchange ideas and apply their knowledge. This approach is influenced by social constructivism, which highlights the role of social interaction and guided learning in knowledge construction (Vygotsky, 1978). I believe that students should actively participate in the learning process rather than simply memorise information presented by the teacher.\n\nHowever, I also recognise that different topics and learners require different teaching approaches. For Grade 6 students, I used flashcards, pictures, demonstrations and familiar classroom objects to make lessons more concrete and understandable. For example, when teaching demonstrative adjectives, I used real classroom objects to help students understand how words such as this, that, these, and those are used in context. In Grade 9, when teaching indirect speech, I first introduced the relevant rules, structures, and notes before providing activities for further practice. This experience taught me the importance of balancing explicit instruction with opportunities for active learning.\n\nReflecting on my practicum, I have learned that effective teaching requires flexibility, patience, clear explanations and continuous reflection. I also recognise the importance of managing classroom behaviour, giving clear instruction  and adapting activities to learners’ abilities. In the future, I aim to improve my teaching strategies by considering students’ individual differences, providing constructive feedback, and designing lessons that encourage independent thinking and collaboration.\n\nUltimately, my goal is to become a reflective and approachable English language teacher who inspires students to develop confidence, communicate effectively, and view learning English as a meaningful and enjoyable experience.\n\n\nReferences :\nRogers, C. R. (1969). Freedom to learn: A view of what education might become. Charles E. Merrill Publishing Company.\nVygotsky, L. S. (1978). Mind in society: The development of higher psychological processes. Harvard University Press.\n",
